{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1717 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7f2117b5d66b93410cdaa5bf689019ea1617af958c0858c9d1205e047b0dedf0" :edb/db-before-t 1716 :edb/db-after-t 1717 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535029 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:10:48.810Z" 13194139535029 true] [17592186220479 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220483 13194139535029 true] [17592186220485 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220489 13194139535029 true] [17592186220491 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220494 13194139535029 true] [17592186220497 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220502 13194139535029 true] [17592186221125 1100 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/ee7115883733062534307c8ff2b1795c77d47d1e8dffeb357dcbe94e07e1f400.png)\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/3883232317dc9ab3e8888fab646fb593ca37585ef3367c5b3000be226962e512.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/ea99ff6ac18f5bb8506edcecfe913b338b64d04e99532f67cec6eb94efc2e9fa.png)\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/d8eaeb8b6abf175f486560cf0d407fd97cd5180907c2db8e6436ccc7e4b09f04.png)" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221125 1100 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n$e(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4$.\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$, the global truncation error is $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the global truncation error is $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the GTE is reduced by a factor of $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n$XXX$" 13194139535029 false] [17592186221125 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p$.\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$, the GTE is\n$e(0.4)|= 4(0.4)^{3} + (0.4)^{4}; = 4 \\cdot 0.064 + 1 \\cdot 0.0256; = 0.256 + 0.0256; = 0.2816; \\approx 0.282$.\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the GTE is\n$e(0.2)|= 4(0.2)^{3} + (0.2)^{4}; = 4 \\cdot 0.008 + 1 \\cdot 0.0016; = 0.032 + 0.0016; = 0.0336; \\approx 0.034$.\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p$, the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n$k^p = 2^p$.\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{p} & \\approx 8.380952… \\\\ p & \\approx \\log_{2} (8.38095…) \\\\ p & \\approx 3.067…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}$.\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad e(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3$." 13194139535029 false] [17592186221125 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =4(0.4)^{3}+(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.064+1\\cdot 0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.256+0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.281\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.282.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+1\\cdot 0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.032+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 2^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈8.380\\,952… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(8.380\\,95…) \\\\ p & ≈3.067….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221126 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24$, the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8$.\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535029 false] [17592186221126 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535029 true] [17592186221126 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}$.\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p$, the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3^{p + 1} & \\approx 80 \\\\ p + 1 & \\approx \\log_{3} (80) \\\\ p + 1 & \\approx 3.987… \\\\ p & \\approx 2.987…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3$." 13194139535029 false] [17592186221126 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.987… \\\\ p & ≈2.987….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221127 1100 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n\n- $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221127 1100 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n2. $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n3. $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" 13194139535029 false] [17592186221127 1145 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535029 true] [17592186221127 1145 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n$e(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0$,\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n$\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0$,\n\nthen the method is of order $p$.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2$.\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}$.\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4$.\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535029 false] [17592186221128 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1$.\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24$.\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1$, what LTE should we expect?\n\n$XXX$" 13194139535029 false] [17592186221128 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24.$\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/31f454d50de05da419a55dfcdd26ac8d7883635e6eb0b95367f9f80fb30643a5.png)" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221128 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}$.\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1$, the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.1\n$$\n\nthe expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015\n$$" 13194139535029 false] [17592186221128 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015.\n$$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221341 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327635 13194139535029 true] [17592186221341 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327642 13194139535029 true] [17592186221341 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327649 13194139535029 true] [17592186221341 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327656 13194139535029 true] [17592186221341 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327681 13194139535029 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327706 13194139535029 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327713 13194139535029 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327720 13194139535029 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327622 13194139535029 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327745 13194139535029 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327752 13194139535029 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327759 13194139535029 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327766 13194139535029 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327769 13194139535029 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327773 13194139535029 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327776 13194139535029 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327779 13194139535029 true] [17592186221918 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535029 true] [17592186221918 1100 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221918 1100 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{2}$\n2. $e(Δx) = 0.3Δx + 2(Δx)^{2}$\n3. $e(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{2} + 4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535029 false] [17592186221918 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3Δx.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is  $9(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535029 true] [17592186221922 1017 #edb/ref 17592186220473 13194139535029 false] [17592186221922 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327629 13194139535029 true] [17592186221922 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535029 true] [17592186221922 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08) = 0.13$.\n- With step size $Δx = 0.02$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02) = 0.002$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139535029 false] [17592186221922 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139535029 true] [17592186221922 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186221923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535029 true] [17592186221923 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n$τ(Δx) = 7(Δx)^{4} - 2(Δx)^{5}$.\nComplete the following sentences. With step size $Δx = 0.5$, the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nWith step size $Δx = 0.25$, the local truncation error is $\\approx$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nDecreasing from $Δx = 0.5$ to $Δx = 0.25$, the LTE is reduced by a factor of $\\approx$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAccording to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535029 false] [17592186221923 1100 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535029 true] [17592186221923 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =7(0.5)^{4}-2(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0.062\\,5-2\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =7(0.25)^{4}-2(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (0.003\\,906…)-2\\cdot (0.000\\,976…) \\\\  & =0.025\\,39… \\\\  & ≈0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.375$ to $0.025\\,39….$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.375}{(0.025\\,39…)}=14.769\\,23…≈14.769.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈14.769\\,23… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(14.769\\,23…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.884… \\\\ p & ≈2.884….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327622 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327623 13194139535029 true] [17592186327622 1089 "q-333693" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327622 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535029 true] [17592186327622 1100 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327622 1113 #uuid "799c4ad6-53eb-5ac5-9b2e-37fa2f8cd5ef" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327622 1145 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327624 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327625 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327626 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327627 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327628 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1041 "selection" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327626 13194139535029 true] [17592186327623 1110 #uuid "1bf4d460-f9a9-54f3-ba46-00f04374de48" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327624 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327624 1111 #uuid "f634b4d1-a4f4-5784-bd1c-4a64e5a541c2" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327625 1034 "I only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327625 1111 #uuid "2aa15dc3-bd0