86327681 1089 "q-334569" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327681 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535029 true] [17592186327681 1100 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.7$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535029 true] [17592186327681 1113 #uuid "c9507c1b-a15f-58d3-89ee-30f737181818" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327681 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.7) & =5(0.7)^{4}-(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.2401-1\\cdot 0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.200\\,5-0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.032\\,43 \\\\  & ≈1.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.007\\,68 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.7$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.7}{0.2}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}=(3.5)^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.032\\,43$ to $0.007\\,68.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.032\\,43}{0.007\\,68}=134.430\\,99…≈134.431.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p} & ≈134.430\\,99… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(134.430\\,99…) \\\\ p & ≈3.912….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327683 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327684 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327685 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327686 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327687 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1041 "field-1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327684 13194139535029 true] [17592186327682 1110 #uuid "e14ceace-04ce-5f64-bd27-04a958bbad06" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327683 1034 "0.207" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327683 1111 #uuid "869b5c1a-98c2-59ef-854d-79794d318318" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327684 1034 "1.032" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327684 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327684 1111 #uuid "f80a6bfa-1d70-5a8b-8aa7-ba429bbfcccf" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327685 1034 "0.514" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327685 1111 #uuid "f4539f9b-d16b-5117-ae70-a49e4b3a7b0b" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327686 1034 "0.008" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327686 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604545