ch that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327761 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327762 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327763 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327764 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327765 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1041 "selection" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327761 13194139535029 true] [17592186327760 1110 #uuid "97edc09a-d4fc-5e52-a3c4-fa4c8f799b9e" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327761 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327761 1111 #uuid "eda904b3-6fe1-59dd-b607-f8948d819c0b" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327762 1034 "I only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327762 1111 #uuid "092df854-b2b4-5c77-a7f0-5876627e1b93" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327763 1034 "III only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327763 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327763 1111 #uuid "1266b73c-6ba8-5d56-89d4-e29b4473f5d7" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327764 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327764 1111 #uuid "1e4e036e-1c3a-528f-8885-894b1ef8a84d" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327765 1034 "II only" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327765 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535029 true] [17592186327765 1111 #uuid "b94d69b0-f450-56f1-82e8-2c14ae3b6a7d" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327766 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327767 13194139535029 true] [17592186327766 1089 "q-334781" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327766 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535029 true] [17592186327766 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327766 1113 #uuid "2a10b1f6-9269-53d3-ade8-daedb2805249" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327766 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327768 13194139535029 true] [17592186327767 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535029 true] [17592186327767 1041 "field-1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327767 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327768 13194139535029 true] [17592186327767 1110 #uuid "f3d11f04-eb9f-53fd-8818-3d2a90fa1664" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327768 1034 "0.002" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327768 1111 #uuid "f0082a3a-8299-5ef6-b970-f615488e202f" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327769 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327770 13194139535029 true] [17592186327769 1089 "q-334796" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327769 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535029 true] [17592186327769 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.15$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.15)≈0.0225.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327769 1113 #uuid "5dcc6b3a-f703-5336-b91c-1153696cc28c" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327769 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.10}=1.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(1.5)^{2}=2.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.0225}{2.25}=0.01.\n$$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327771 13194139535029 true] [17592186327770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327772 13194139535029 true] [17592186327770 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535029 true] [17592186327770 1041 "field-1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327770 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327772 13194139535029 true] [17592186327770 1110 #uuid "06a3953a-69eb-5039-b896-19ef58a264cd" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327771 1034 "0.1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327771 1111 #uuid "bb075d45-059a-5c30-acad-226263ddce95" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327772 1034 "0.01" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327772 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327772 1111 #uuid "b1872365-4257-544a-9736-ee9615954847" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327773 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327774 13194139535029 true] [17592186327773 1089 "q-334827" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327773 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535029 true] [17592186327773 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.18$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.18)≈0.2025.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.04,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.04)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327773 1113 #uuid "276d68fa-4cd1-5416-b700-dfeb8978eba6" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327773 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.04}=4.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(4.5)^{2}=20.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.04,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.04)≈\\frac{0.2025}{20.25}=0.01.\n$$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327774 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327775 13194139535029 true] [17592186327774 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535029 true] [17592186327774 1041 "field-1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327774 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327775 13194139535029 true] [17592186327774 1110 #uuid "7bb172d8-b607-5d07-9b84-e6786163d41f" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327775 1034 "0.01" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327775 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327775 1111 #uuid "f6bfd448-8c77-542e-913b-b1d82ce527a8" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327776 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327777 13194139535029 true] [17592186327776 1089 "q-334946" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327776 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535029 true] [17592186327776 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327776 1113 #uuid "d4cf6dc3-6e78-578e-a0e6-3564f50cd0db" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327776 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327777 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327778 13194139535029 true] [17592186327777 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535029 true] [17592186327777 1041 "field-1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327777 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327778 13194139535029 true] [17592186327777 1110 #uuid "59973590-9335-59e3-b25b-131f52b69d9a" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327778 1034 "0.05" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327778 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327778 1111 #uuid "059cf0dc-c6e7-518f-b6be-7aac96e2ac41" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327779 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327780 13194139535029 true] [17592186327779 1089 "q-334948" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327779 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535029 true] [17592186327779 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.108.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327779 1113 #uuid "ead683db-5726-5670-bf85-2080e4a803bb" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327779 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2+1}={3}^{3}=27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.108}{27}=0.004.\n$$" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327781 13194139535029 true] [17592186327780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327782 13194139535029 true] [17592186327780 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535029 true] [17592186327780 1041 "field-1" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327780 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327782 13194139535029 true] [17592186327780 1110 #uuid "a21c3182-2cc2-508b-9dd7-d3980c60c0c5" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327781 1034 "0.04" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327781 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327781 1111 #uuid "86b1ad47-a165-51f7-a7ca-5284bb890409" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327782 1034 "0.004" 13194139535029 true] [17592186327782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535029 true] [17592186327782 1111 #uuid "1df165d1-2468-5ad7-ab0d-3674062ac289" 13194139535029 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-333693" 17592186327622 "q-333693/selection" 17592186327623 "q-333693/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" 17592186327624 "q-333693/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" 17592186327625 "q-333693/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" 17592186327626 "q-333693/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" 17592186327627 "q-333693/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" 17592186327628 "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c" 17592186327629 "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" 17592186327630 "q-333702/selection/ma-v